1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:08,664
Before Malika sold a single train set online, she sat at her kitchen table one Sunday night and signed her toy shop up with the bank.

2
00:00:08,714 --> 00:00:18,264
Lesson 15 finished the card zone, so from here the course builds the shop's side, starting with the portal that Malika and the bank's staff used in Lesson 1.

3
00:00:18,314 --> 00:00:21,648
This lesson is about her first five minutes in it.

4
00:00:21,698 --> 00:00:34,056
The page said "Create a merchant account", and under it, "We will email you a code to confirm the address." There were three boxes: company name, work email and password.

5
00:00:34,106 --> 00:00:36,864
She filled them in and pressed Create account.

6
00:00:36,914 --> 00:00:42,600
Her password went straight to the login server, the one place that checks passwords.

7
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:46,200
It never touched ecom's own database.

8
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:49,656
ecom saved her shop as pending, and nothing more.

9
00:00:49,706 --> 00:00:51,528
Then her phone buzzed.

10
00:00:51,578 --> 00:00:54,792
An email had arrived, with six digits in it.

11
00:00:54,842 --> 00:00:56,664
Why a code?

12
00:00:56,714 --> 00:01:00,960
Because anyone can type anyone's email into a sign-up form.

13
00:01:01,010 --> 00:01:12,000
A stranger could open a shop with Malika's address, and the bank would send contracts, reports and payout notices to an inbox whose owner never asked for any of it.

14
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:16,152
The code asks one thing: is this inbox really yours?

15
00:01:16,202 --> 00:01:21,360
It is a one-time code: a short number that works once, for a short time.

16
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:24,936
Only someone who can read that inbox can type it back.

17
00:01:24,986 --> 00:01:30,072
The store that keeps the codes never keeps the code itself, or the email.

18
00:01:30,122 --> 00:01:36,960
It keeps only fingerprints of them: enough to check a match, useless to anyone who steals them.

19
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:44,376
In the portal, the page now said "Check your email." It asked for the code, and said it works for a few minutes.

20
00:01:44,426 --> 00:01:48,600
While the box was not full, the Verify button stayed grey.

21
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:56,304
Under it, "Send a new code" counted down the seconds before it could be pressed again, so nobody can flood an inbox.

22
00:01:56,354 --> 00:02:01,056
Malika typed the six digits, and Verify lit up.

23
00:02:01,106 --> 00:02:02,928
One click.

24
00:02:02,978 --> 00:02:10,488
The page said "Email verified", told her to sign in, and said the bank will now check her documents.

25
00:02:10,538 --> 00:02:16,872
That right code is email verification: proof that the person controls the address.

26
00:02:16,922 --> 00:02:24,552
Behind that screen, Malika became her shop's first admin, both in ecom and on the login server.

27
00:02:24,602 --> 00:02:26,424
And the stranger?

28
00:02:26,474 --> 00:02:28,440
Their wrong guesses are counted.

29
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:35,112
Too many, and the sign-up is locked until the window ends, even if a new code is asked for.

30
00:02:35,162 --> 00:02:36,984
That is a lockout.

31
00:02:37,034 --> 00:02:40,104
Every refusal says when to try again.

32
00:02:40,154 --> 00:02:43,584
One more case, and it is the one I care about most.

33
00:02:43,634 --> 00:02:49,224
If the store that keeps the codes is down, nothing is sent and nothing is accepted.

34
00:02:49,274 --> 00:02:56,880
Most teams get this wrong under pressure: sign-ups are stuck, and someone lets them through just this once.

35
00:02:56,930 --> 00:03:00,384
That is exactly how an unchecked shop gets in.

36
00:03:00,434 --> 00:03:06,464
So what should a sign-up do when the store that keeps the codes is down?

37
00:03:06,514 --> 00:03:10,040
So the system fails closed, never open.

38
00:03:10,090 --> 00:03:13,304
The code protects the real owner of the address.

39
00:03:13,354 --> 00:03:19,352
It protects the bank too, because every later message reaches the person who really asked.

40
00:03:19,402 --> 00:03:21,632
Malika never noticed any of it.

41
00:03:21,682 --> 00:03:24,872
She saw six digits, and a green tick.

42
00:03:24,922 --> 00:03:31,544
To recap: ecom sends a one-time code to the inbox and keeps only its fingerprint.

43
00:03:31,594 --> 00:03:36,928
So what proves that Malika owns the address?

44
00:03:36,978 --> 00:03:39,568
Only the code that reached her inbox.

45
00:03:39,618 --> 00:03:43,264
Next: how her shop earns the right to take money.
